#
# Arithmetic in BusyBox ash: a name whose value is a name, all the way down to
# one that is not set.
#
# A name-shaped value is read again as an expression, so with `x=abc` the
# expression `x` is whatever `abc` is. Two questions come out of that and the
# panel answers them in three ways. dash does not recurse at all — `Illegal
# number: abc`, and `dash/arith.tests` is where that lives. ksh93 recurses and
# refuses an unset name at the bottom with its `set -u` sentence. bash, zsh
# and this shell recurse and take the unset name as zero.
#
# Not alone, then, and not core either: dash's refusal keeps it out of the
# tier every column has to agree on. It is here because this column is the
# one that got it wrong. The recursion was answered for this dialect and the
# unset name at the bottom was not, so every row below stopped the script
# with "the shells disagree here and no dialect was chosen" (2026-09-16).

x=abc
unset abc
printf 'bare:          %s\n' "$(( x ))"
printf 'in a sum:      %s\n' "$(( x + 1 ))"
printf 'parenthesized: %s\n' "$(( (x) * 2 ))"

# Two names deep, so the zero is the bottom's and not the first lookup's.
p=q
q=r
unset r
printf 'two deep:      %s\n' "$(( p ))"

# The controls. A set name at the bottom is its value, which says the rows
# above recursed rather than reading `abc` as zero on sight; and a name
# written in the expression itself is zero everywhere on the panel.
abc=5
printf 'set at the bottom: %s\n' "$(( x + 1 ))"
unset nosuch_zz
printf 'unset at the top:  %s\n' "$(( nosuch_zz + 1 ))"
